  "member_id": "el-manira-abdel",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B006",
        "laureates": [
          "Jacques F. A. P. Miller",
          "Max D. Cooper"
        ],
        "citation": "for discoveries of distinct T- and B-lymphocyte lineages that established the organization of adaptive immunity"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B012",
    "B019"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B006 most convincingly combines fundamental explanatory reach, decades of confirmation, and a creditable achievement bounded to two complementary discoveries. Miller's thymus-dependent lineage work and Cooper's delineation of the complementary B-cell lineage establish the distinct T- and B-lymphocyte framework, without requiring the prize to claim the subsequent development of all cellular immunology or its applications. This narrow formulation directly addresses the attribution concern: it recognizes the specified lineage discoveries rather than a diffuse collective history.",
    "case_against": "The historical development of lymphocyte-lineage biology included other important investigators, and the long interval since the discoveries warrants careful comparison with later medically transformative work. The proposed citation can be defended only if it remains confined to the establishment of distinct T- and B-cell lineages and does not imply credit for later advances in vaccination, immunotherapy, or broader adaptive-immunity research.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson favors B012 because identifying CFTR gave cystic fibrosis a causal molecular explanation and a mature diagnostic and therapeutic legacy, while retaining Collins, Riordan, and Tsui as a three-person representation of the linked achievement.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B012 as an exceptionally strong alternative. However, the proposed CFTR allocation must represent a broad collaborative sequence of mapping, cloning, and characterization. By contrast, B006 can be framed around two distinct, complementary lineage discoveries, with a citation narrow enough to avoid appropriating the contributions of other early investigators. That clearer boundary preserves B006 as my preference."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "Perlmann argues that B006 is strongest if its citation carefully separates Miller's and Cooper's complementary discoveries from later immunological advances and contributions by other early investigators.",
        "response": "I agree, and this is why the configuration is unchanged but the citation remains deliberately limited. Crediting discoveries of distinct T- and B-lymphocyte lineages identifies the specific organizing framework attributable to the proposed pair; it neither assigns them all of cellular immunology nor asserts credit for the later medical applications that followed."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "svenningsson-per",
        "point": "Svenningsson recognizes the extraordinary population-level benefit of B021 but identifies unresolved boundaries among peptide biology, physiology, pharmacological optimization, industrial development, and translation.",
        "response": "B021 has exceptional therapeutic significance, but its proposed achievement combines several scientific and translational stages whose allocation within a laureate limit remains difficult. The mature and sharply delimited biological principle in B006 has greater conceptual finality and a more defensible exact credit assignment."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record can support the proposed narrow B006 wording without underrepresenting other early contributors to lymphocyte-lineage biology.",
      "Whether the disease-specific causal explanation and direct medical trajectory of B012 should receive greater comparative weight than B006's broader foundational organization of adaptive immunity."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "The supplied profile records coauthorship with Thomas Perlmann, a fellow member, but no documented connection to the proposed B006 laureates or candidate-specific conflict; no recusal is apparent."
  }
}
